Yes. Mathematica sorts terms in `Plus` by default (`Orderless`), so the displayed order usually won’t match the input/order of derivation.

A workaround is to temporarily remove `Orderless`, expand, then restore it:

```mathematica
myExpand[expr_] := Module[{tmp},
  Unprotect[Plus];
  ClearAttributes[Plus, Orderless];
  tmp = HoldForm @@ {Expand[expr] /. a_Plus :> Reverse[a]};
  SetAttributes[Plus, Orderless];
  tmp
]
```

Then use:

```mathematica
lapla12 // myExpand
```

This preserves a more natural term order in the output. For large expressions, this is often easier than trying to manually sort terms afterward.

You can’t really “change Mathematica’s canonical order” globally for this, but you can display terms in a custom order.

Use `Row`, `SortBy`, and a priority function for factors and terms. For example:

```wl
prodSort[mono_] :=
 If[Head[mono] =!= Times, Row[{mono}],
  Module[{termLst = List @@ mono, priorityTable},
   priorityTable = Piecewise[{
       {0, NumericQ[#]},
       {10, Not@FreeQ[#, e]},
       {11, Not@FreeQ[#, A]},
       {100, Not@FreeQ[#, SuperPlus[\[Phi]]]},
       {101, Not@FreeQ[#, SuperMinus[\[Phi]]]}
     }, 50] & /@ termLst;
   Row @ SortBy[Transpose[{termLst, priorityTable}], Last][[All, 1]]
  ]
 ]

sumSort[polynom_] :=
 Module[{termLst = List @@ polynom, priorityTable},
  priorityTable = Piecewise[{
      {0, Not@FreeQ[#, h]},
      {100, Not@FreeQ[#, SuperPlus[\[Phi]]]},
      {100, Not@FreeQ[#, SuperMinus[\[Phi]]]}
    }, 10] & /@ termLst;
  SortBy[Transpose[{termLst, priorityTable}], Last][[All, 1]] // 
    Riffle[#, "+"] & // Row
 ]
```

Then apply:

```wl
sumSort[prodSort /@ expr] // TraditionalForm
```

This is mainly for presentation, not algebraic rewriting.

This is likely not a Mathematica vs. PlayerPro difference, but a version difference: Mathematica 8 and PlayerPro 8 show the older dialog, while Mathematica 9 shows the newer one.

To get the same dialog in PlayerPro, specify the file filter explicitly:

```wolfram
Button["Ok",
  SystemDialogInput["FileSave",
    {".PDF", {"PDF-Files" -> {"*.pdf"}, "All Files" -> {"*"}}}
  ]
]
```

That forces the newer-style dialog behavior in both Mathematica and PlayerPro.

This is a known PDF export artifact with transparent RegionPlot output in Mathematica: the little triangles come from the vector backend, not from your plot itself.

A practical workaround is to force rasterization of the opaque region during export by combining the plot with an invisible textured polygon:

```mathematica
regionplot = RegionPlot[
  x^2 + y^2 < 1, {x, -1, 1}, {y, -1, 1},
  PlotStyle -> Opacity[0.5, Black]
];

rasterTrick[plot_] := Show[plot,
  Prolog -> {
    Opacity[0],
    Texture[{{{0, 0, 0, 0}}}],
    VertexTextureCoordinates -> {{0, 0}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}},
    Polygon[{{0, 0}, {.1, 0}, {.1, .1}}]
  }
];

Export["region.pdf", rasterTrick[regionplot]]
```

This usually removes the unwanted black lines in the PDF.

For Dynamic Time Warping visualization in Mathematica/Wolfram Language, the same idea applies: turn each time slice into a list of points, then animate a sequence of plots.

Example structure:

data = {
  {{0.2, 42.8}, {0.4, 12.3}, {0.6, 32.1}, {0.8, 37.3}},
  {{0.2, 44.2}, {0.4, 17.8}, {0.6, 39.0}, {0.8, 30.1}}
};

Then:

ListAnimate[
  ListLinePlot[#, PlotRange -> {10, 50}] & /@ data
]

Key point: use a fixed PlotRange so the graph doesn’t rescale between frames. If your CSV has a different layout, import it and reshape it into this nested-list form first.

This is the same preemptive/evaluation-order issue as the linked thread: the `EventHandler` runs before the front end has fully updated the field, so `SelectionMove` or other side effects can happen too early.

A common fix is to defer the post-processing with `DynamicWrapper` and a flag:

```wl
DynamicModule[{thing = "foo", doMove = False, nb = EvaluationNotebook[]},
 DynamicWrapper[
  EventHandler[
   InputField[Dynamic[thing], String],
   {"ReturnKeyDown" :> (thing = thing <> "bar"; doMove = True)}
  ],
  If[doMove,
   doMove = False;
   SelectionMove[nb, Next, Word]
  ],
  SynchronousUpdating -> False
 ]
]
```

If your issue is “input field is not defined” at the moment you compute, the same idea applies: set a flag in the event handler, then do the computation in the wrapper or a separate queued dynamic after the control exists/updates.

In Mathematica, `Plot` can take a list of functions, so you can plot multiple curves in the same graph.

Example:

```mathematica
f1[x_] := x^2;
f2[x_] := x^4;

Manipulate[
  Plot[{f1[x + n], f2[x + n]}, {x, 0.0001, 1}, PlotRange -> {-10, 10}],
  {n, 1, 10}
]
```

If your function “returns multiple values,” make it return a list, then plot the list of expressions the same way.

If you want, I can also show how to plot a function that returns `{y1, y2}` directly.

You’re losing “decimals” only in the display/export formatting, not in the underlying values.

In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, `Export[..., "Table"]` writes numbers using a default textual format. If you want a fixed number of displayed digits, format the exported list first, e.g.:

```wl
time = Table[i, {i, 0, 0.4, 0.01}];
time2 = NumberForm[#, {Infinity, 6}] & /@ time;
op = time^2 + time;

Export["data.dat", Transpose@{time2, op}, "Table"]
```

Or better, use `NumberString`/`ToString[NumberForm[...]]` if you need plain text formatting.

Also note: `NumberForm` changes presentation, not numeric precision. If you need exact stored decimal text, format before export.

Use `Graphics` + `Inset` instead of `GraphicsGrid` if you need the plot regions to line up exactly. `GraphicsGrid` aligns the full boxed area, including labels and legends, so the axes can shift.

Example:

```mathematica
figa = ListContourPlot[data1, Frame -> True];
figb = ListContourPlot[data2, Frame -> True];
figc = ListContourPlot[data3, Frame -> True];

Graphics[
 {
  Inset[figa, {0.17, 0.25}, Automatic, 0.30],
  Inset[figb, {0.50, 0.25}, Automatic, 0.30],
  Inset[figc, {0.83, 0.25}, Automatic, 0.30]
 },
 PlotRange -> {{0, 1}, {0, 0.5}}
]
```

Adjust the inset positions and sizes until the axes line up. If needed, add legends/labels with `Inset[Text[...], ...]`.

If you have a matrix like

m = {{a,b,c}, {d,e,f}, {g,h,i}};

use Total to add across rows or down columns.

Add down columns:
Total[m, {1}]
gives {a + d + g, b + e + h, c + f + i}

Add across rows:
Total[m, {2}]
gives {a + b + c, d + e + f, g + h + i}

So Total with the second argument controls which level is summed.

In Mathematica, a clean way is to build a lookup on the year-month-day part of the daily list, then map it onto the hourly list.

Example:

dailyRules = Association[
   Take[First[#], 3] -> Rest[#] & /@ daily
];

merged = Map[
   Join[#, Lookup[dailyRules, Take[First[#], 3], {}]] &,
   hourly
];

This matches on `{year, month, day}` and appends the daily values to each hourly record.

If you want the more compact rule-based version from the resolved thread, that also works well using `Dispatch`, `Take`, and `Join`.

Yes. Convert each 2D list into 3D coordinates by placing one plot in the x-y plane and the other in the x-z plane, then plot them together.

Example:

```wl
data = Table[{x, Sin[x]}, {x, 0, 5 Pi, Pi/20}];

ListPointPlot3D[
  {
    data /. {x_?NumericQ, y_?NumericQ} :> {x, y, 0},
    data /. {x_?NumericQ, y_?NumericQ} :> {x, 0, y}
  },
  PlotStyle -> {Blue, Darker@Green},
  AxesOrigin -> {0, 0, 0},
  Boxed -> False, Ticks -> None, AxesStyle -> LightGray
] /. Point -> Line
```

If your two lists are different, just apply the same idea to each list separately. If you want, I can adapt this to your exact data format.

To make two plots have the same width, set ImageSize in a way that fixes the overall width, and keep Plot/ImagePadding consistent.

For example, use the same width for both:

With[{w = 550},
 Row[{
   Show[plot1, ImageSize -> w],
   Show[plot2, ImageSize -> w]
 }]
]

If you want the visible plot areas aligned, also match padding:

ImagePadding -> {{60, 20}, {60, 20}}

In your resolved thread, the key trick was using:

ImageSize -> {Automatic, 550}

This keeps the height fixed while letting Mathematica compute the width consistently. If one plot has a colorbar or different labels, it may still look narrower unless you equalize the paddings.

Yes. Store the points in a list and update a single plot variable inside the loop.

Example:

```mathematica
list = {};
p = {};
Dynamic[p]

Do[
  list = Append[list, {i, j, k}];
  p = ListPointPlot3D[list],
  {i, 10}, {j, 10}, {k, 5}
]
```

`Dynamic[p]` keeps one plot visible and refreshes it whenever `p` changes. This way, new points are added to the same plot instead of opening new windows.

If you want, I can also show a cleaner version using `Sow`/`Reap` or `DynamicModule`.

If you want the loop results stored in a list, don’t use `Print`; `Print` returns `Null`. Use `Sow`/`Reap`, or better, replace the loops with `Table`.

Example with `Sow`/`Reap`:

v = {{0, 0, 0}, {1, 0, 0}, {1, 0, 1}, {0, 1, 1}, {1, 1, 1}};

result = Reap[
  For[i = 1, i <= Length[v], i++,
    For[e = 1, e <= Length[v], e++,
      Sow[Mod[v[[i]] + v[[e]], 2]]
    ]
  ]
][[2, 1]];

v = Join[v, result]

Or more simply:

v = Join[v, Flatten[Table[Mod[v[[i]] + v[[e]], 2], {i, 5}, {e, 5}], 1]]

Use `Sow`/`Reap` to collect values instead of printing them. Also, `Do` is usually better than `For` because it localizes the iterator.

Example pattern:

```mathematica
Reap[
  Do[
    If[condition,
      Sow[i*StepSize1]
    ],
    {i, 0, 999}
  ]
][[2, 1]]
```

For your nested loop, replace the `Print[...]` with `Sow[...]`:

```mathematica
Reap[
  Do[
    Do[
      If[Abs[Nest[f, i*StepSize1, n] - 0.51304] < 0.0001,
        Sow[i*StepSize1];
        Break[]
      ],
      {n, 100, 599}
    ],
    {i, 0, 999}
  ]
][[2, 1]]
```

This returns a list you can use with `ListPlot`.

If you want the output from a `While` loop in a list, use `AppendTo` or, better, `Reap`/`Sow`.

Example with `Reap`/`Sow`:

```mathematica
result = Reap[
  i = 1;
  While[i < 3,
    outdata = ReadList[test[[i]], Record, RecordSeparators -> {{">"}, {" "}}];
    Sow[outdata];
    i++
  ]
][[2, 1]]
```

`result` will be a list of the `outdata` values from each loop iteration.

If you only need a simple collection, `Table` is often cleaner than `While`.

Use a list variable and append each `NMinimize` result inside the loop.

Example:

```wl
results = {};
Do[
  AppendTo[results,
    NMinimize[{f[x], constraints}, x]
  ],
  {i, 1, n}
]
```

If you want a nested list with only the solution part:

```wl
solutions = Table[
  NMinimize[{f[x], constraints}, x],
  {n}
];
```

For your previous pattern, you can also store each iteration’s variable values with `StepMonitor`:

```wl
s = {};
NMinimize[
  {objectiveFunction, And @@ Thread[vars1 >= 0] && Total[vars1] == 100 && vars1 ∈ Integers},
  vars1,
  StepMonitor :> AppendTo[s, vars1]
]
```

Then `s` is the list of iterates.

If you want a nested list of `NMinimize` results, use `Table` or `Map` and keep each result as an element of the list.

Example with a fixed set of parameter pairs:

```wl
pairs = {{3, 1}, {4, 2}, {5, 4}, {4, 3}, {6, 1}, {6, 2}, {6, 3}, {6, 5}};

sols = Table[
   NMinimize[
     expr[i, j, x, y],
     {x, y}
   ],
   { {i, j}, pairs}
];
```

This gives a list like:

```wl
{{min1, rules1}, {min2, rules2}, ...}
```

If you want it nested by one index:

```wl
sols = Table[
   NMinimize[expr[i, j, x, y], {x, y}],
   {i, {3, 4, 5, 4, 6, 6, 6, 6}},
   {j, {1, 2, 4, 3, 1, 2, 3, 5}}
];
```

Then `sols[[1,1]]`, etc. access each solution.

If you want a sharp-edged “smooth” histogram, you can use `SmoothHistogram` with a very small kernel width or bandwidth, but keep in mind it will still be smoothed unless you make it extremely narrow.

Example combining both:

data1 = RandomVariate[NormalDistribution[0, 1], 100];
data2 = RandomVariate[NormalDistribution[1, 1], 100];

Show[
  Histogram[data1, Automatic, "PDF"],
  SmoothHistogram[data2, PlotTheme -> "Detailed"],
  PlotRange -> All
]

If you want the `Histogram` and `SmoothHistogram` heights to match, use `"PDF"` for the histogram. If you mean a truly sharp boundary, `Histogram` is the right choice; `SmoothHistogram` inherently rounds edges.

Yes. Use FrameTicks with explicit position -> label rules.

Example:

MatrixPlot[list,
 FrameTicks -> {
   {{1, 2, 3}, None},
   {{{1, 101}, {2, 167}, {3, 188}, {4, 205}}, None}
 }]

For arbitrary strings, just replace the labels:

MatrixPlot[list,
 FrameTicks -> {
   {{1, 2, 3}, None},
   {{{1, "Alice"}, {2, "Bob"}, {3, "Carla"}, {4, "David"}}, None}
 }]

In general, FrameTicks can map tick positions to any text or formatted expression.

`Ticks` controls the axes; when you use `Frame -> True`, you need `FrameTicks` instead.

For example:

```wl
xticks = {#, StringReplace[ToString[# σ], " " -> ""]} & /@ Range[-6, 6];

Plot[PDF[NormalDistribution[0, 1.5], x], {x, -7, 7},
  Frame -> True,
  FrameTicks -> {{Automatic, None}, {xticks, xticks}}
]
```

So the issue is just that `Frame` and `Ticks` use different options.

Yes. Since one family is plotted with swapped axes, the cleanest approach is to transform that plot and overlay it on the first one.

In Mathematica:

```wl
f[t_, t0_] := -(2 - 4/Pi*ArcTan[2])*Exp[-t]*(t - t0);
g[x_, x0_] := (x - x0)/(-(2 - 4/Pi*ArcTan[x + 2]));

s1 = Show@Table[
   Plot[f[t, t0], {t, 0, 1}, PlotRange -> {0, -0.3},
    AxesLabel -> {"t", "x"}, PlotStyle -> Red],
   {t0, 0, 1, 0.1}
];

s2 = Show@Table[
   Plot[g[x, x0], {x, 0, -0.3}, PlotRange -> {0, 1},
    AxesLabel -> {"x", "t"}],
   {x0, 0, -0.3, -0.05}
];

trF = GeometricTransformation[#,
   Composition[ReflectionTransform[{-1, 0}], RotationTransform[Pi/2]]] &;

Show[s1, Graphics@(trF @@ s2)]
```

This rotates and reflects the second plot so both share the same horizontal axis.

Yes. If the step is fixed, avoid iterating `DatePlus` repeatedly. Compute the time increment once, then multiply:

```wl
dt = {{1, "Day"}, {5, "Week"}};
n = 10000;
start = {2008, 1, 1};

absDt = DatePlus[0, dt];
future = AbsoluteTime[start] + (n - 1) absDt;
end = DateList[future]
```

This is much faster because it does one date conversion and one arithmetic operation instead of `n-1` date additions.

For your example, this is essentially the same as the resolved thread’s fastest solution and should be far quicker than `Nest[DatePlus[#, step]&, start, n - 1]`.

`ExportForm[..., "gif"]` won’t work inside `Manipulate` that way. `ExportForm` produces an export-ready object, not an inline animated GIF widget.

Use a `DynamicModule`/`Panel` style display and export the animation separately, or build the GIF first and then show it with `Import`.

Example:

```wl
pic1 = Export["sine.gif", Table[Plot[Sin[n x], {x, 0, Pi}], {n, 0, 2, 0.1}]];
pic2 = Export["cosine.gif", Table[Plot[Cos[n x], {x, 0, Pi}], {n, 0, 2, 0.1}]];

CloudDeploy[
 Manipulate[
  Import[Switch[pic, "sine", "sine.gif", "cosine", "cosine.gif"]],
  {pic, {"sine", "cosine"}}
 ]
]
```

If you want everything generated on the fly, use `Switch[pic, ...]` to select the list of frames, then `ListAnimate` or `Animate` rather than `ExportForm`.

Use `Table` to generate the animation frames instead of exporting `Manipulate` directly. `Export` works best with a list of graphics.

Example:

```wl
mov = Table[
  Plot[Sin[2 x + ϕ], {x, 0, 10}, PlotRange -> All],
  {ϕ, 0, 2 Pi, 2 Pi/10}
];

Export["movie.gif", mov]
```

This gives a forward-only animation and works for GIF export. You can make it smoother by using a smaller step size, e.g. `2 Pi/50`.

Yes. `ListDensityPlot` expects gridded data, so for scattered points you usually bin first.

A fast approach is to create a 2D histogram/sparse array, aggregate values per bin, then plot:

```wl
d = (* your {x,y,z} data *);

zvalues = Log10@d[[All, 3]];
epsilon = 1*^-10;
indexes = 1 + Floor[(1 - epsilon) 512 Rescale[d[[All, {1, 2}]]]];

SetSystemOptions["SparseArrayOptions" -> {"TreatRepeatedEntries" -> (Total[{##}] &)}];
binmeansZ = SparseArray[indexes -> zvalues];
SetSystemOptions["SparseArrayOptions" -> {"TreatRepeatedEntries" -> First}];

MatrixPlot[GaussianFilter[binmeansZ\[Transpose], 2],
  DataRange -> {{-2, 2}, {-2, 2}}]
```

If you want true discrete bins, skip `GaussianFilter`. If you want, I can show a version using `BinCounts` or `ListDensityPlot` directly from scattered points.

For discrete noisy data, a good approach is:

1. denoise/smooth first, then  
2. detect extrema on the cleaned list.

In Mathematica, wavelet denoising is one option:

```wl
temptimelist = Range[200]/10;
data = Sinc[temptimelist];
noise = RandomReal[{-0.02, 0.02}, 200];
dwd = DiscreteWaveletTransform[data + noise, SymletWavelet[7], 6];
dwd = WaveletThreshold[dwd];
est = InverseWaveletTransform[dwd];
```

Then find extrema positions from the sign changes in `Differences`:

```wl
findExtremaPos[list_List] := Module[{signs, extremaPos, minPos, maxPos},
  signs = Sign[Differences[list]];
  signs = signs //. {a___, q_, 0, z__} -> {a, q, q, z};
  extremaPos = 1 + Accumulate@(Length /@ Split[signs]);
  If[First@signs == 1,
    {extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]], extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]]},
    {extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]], extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]]}
  ]
]
```

If you only need peaks, `FindPeaks[est, threshold]` is also useful.

If you have a list or table and want the position of the maximum/minimum value, use `Position` with `Max`/`Min`.

Example:

```mathematica
tt1 = Table[
   z /. Solve[z^2 == x^2 y - z, z, Method -> Reduce],
   {x, 0, 5, 1}, {y, 0, 5, 1}
];

max = Max[tt1];
pos = First@Position[tt1, max];
```

To convert that position into the corresponding `{x, y}` pair:

```mathematica
Table[{x, y}, {x, 0, 5, 1}, {y, 0, 5, 1}][[Sequence @@ pos[[;; 2]]]]
```

This returns the value and its location, e.g.:

```mathematica
1/2 (-1 + Sqrt[501])
{5, 5}
```

For a minimum, just replace `Max` with `Min`.

In web-embedded CDF/CDF Preview, `Hyperlink` and `SystemOpen` often won’t behave like in desktop Mathematica. The workaround is to use JLink and Java’s `Desktop.browse`, which hands the URL to the system browser:

```wl
Manipulate[
 Button["Browse Me", desktop@browse[uri]],
 Initialization :> (
   Needs["JLink`"]; InstallJava[];
   LoadJavaClass["java.awt.Desktop"];
   desktop = java`awt`Desktop`getDesktop[];
   uri = JavaNew["java.net.URI", "http://example.com"];
 ),
 SaveDefinitions -> True
]
```

This doesn’t explicitly request a “new tab”; it opens the URL in the default browser, and the browser decides whether that means a new tab or window. That’s why it works in the embedded CDF context.

Use `FindFit` on the numerical solution from `NDSolve`, but fit the **ODE right-hand side** rather than `y[x]` itself.

Example:

```wl
F[x_] := If[x < 37.5, 2.80 + 0.0036 x, 117.86 - 6.314 x + 0.0865 x^2];

sol = NDSolve[{y'[x] == -(0.0595 + 3716/(11780 + y[x])) y[x], 
   y[0] == 650000}, y, {x, 0, 60}];

solY = y /. First[sol];

data = Table[{solY[x], F[x], solY'[x]}, {x, 0, 60, 1}];

fit = FindFit[data, -(a + b/(c + d v1 + e v2)), {a, b, c, d, e}, {v1, v2}]
```

This treats `v1 = y[x]` and `v2 = F[x]`, with target `y'[x]`.

Note: because the model is nonlinear, `FindFit` may return only a local optimum. If the fit is poor, try better initial guesses, rescaling variables, or `NMinimize`/`NonlinearModelFit`.

A practical way is to turn the ODE fitting into a parameter-estimation problem and use the data directly.

In Mathematica:

```wl
{tvals, xvals, yvals} = Transpose[data];
{xdata, ydata} = (Transpose[{tvals, #}]) & /@ {xvals, yvals};

fit = ParametricNDSolveValue[
  {
    x'[t] == a x[t] - b x[t] y[t],
    y'[t] == -c y[t] + d x[t] y[t],
    x[t0] == x0, y[t0] == y0
  },
  {x, y}, {t, Min[tvals], Max[tvals]},
  {a, b, c, d, x0, y0}
];

obj = Total[
  (fit[tvals][[1]] - xvals)^2 + (fit[tvals][[2]] - yvals)^2
];

NMinimize[obj, {a, b, c, d, x0, y0}]
```

If convergence is poor, use good starting guesses and possibly smooth/interpolate the data first. For this kind of model, `FindFit` with `NDSolve`/`ParametricNDSolveValue` is usually the right approach.

Yes. If you mean fitting parameters in a parametric ODE to data, use NDSolve with the parameters as unknowns and minimize the mismatch.

Typical workflow:
1. Write the ODE with parameters, e.g. a, b.
2. Solve numerically for given parameter values.
3. Define an error function against your data.
4. Use FindMinimum / NMinimize / NonlinearModelFit to estimate parameters.

Example pattern:

data = {{t1, y1}, {t2, y2}, ...};

ySol[a_, b_] := NDSolveValue[
  {y'[t] == a - t + b y[t], y[0] == 1},
  y, {t, 0, 1}
];

err[a_, b_] := Total[(ySol[a, b] /@ data[[All, 1]] - data[[All, 2]])^2];

FindMinimum[err[a, b], {a, 1}, {b, 4}]

If you want, I can show the exact Mathematica code for your specific ODE and data format.

A practical way is to separate the unknown initial conditions and parameters, then fit the ODE solution to the data with `NMinimize` or `FindFit` over the ODE solution.

For example:

```wl
data = {{11,45.79,41.4}, ...};
tvals = data[[All,1]];
xvals = data[[All,2]];
yvals = data[[All,3]];

sol = NDSolveValue[
  {
    x'[t] == a x[t] - b x[t] y[t],
    y'[t] == -c y[t] + d x[t] y[t],
    x[11] == x0, y[11] == y0
  },
  {x, y}, {t, 11, 40},
  {a, b, c, d, x0, y0}
];
```

Then minimize the residual:

```wl
obj = Total[(sol[[1]] /@ tvals - xvals)^2 + (sol[[2]] /@ tvals - yvals)^2];

NMinimize[{obj, b > 0 && c > 0 && d > 0}, {a,b,c,d,x0,y0}]
```

If you want a constraint, include it directly in `NMinimize`, e.g. `a + b == 1` or `b > 0`.

If the value is in a string, first extract it, then assign it to a symbol.

In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, use string parsing functions like StringCases, StringSplit, or RegularExpression, then ToExpression if you want the result evaluated.

Example:

x = "value=42";
n = ToExpression @ StringCases[x, RegularExpression["\\d+"]][[1]];
y = n;

If you mean replacing part of a string-based variable name, that’s different: symbols aren’t usually reassigned from inside strings directly. You’d typically use Symbol["x"] to refer to a symbol by name.

Example:

s = "x";
Symbol[s] = 10;

This assigns 10 to x.

For NIntegrate, the best speedup is often not parallelism but disabling symbolic preprocessing:

Method -> {Automatic, "SymbolicProcessing" -> 0}

Example:

ParallelTable[
  NIntegrate[f[x], {x, 0, 10}, Method -> {"SymbolicProcessing" -> 0}],
  {f, {x^2, x, 3 x}}
]

Or without ParallelTable:

Table[
  NIntegrate[f[x], {x, 0, 10}, Method -> {"SymbolicProcessing" -> 0}],
  {f, {x^2, x, 3 x}}
]

For your complicated integrand, try that first. If you still want parallelism, ParallelTable can work, but NIntegrate itself usually benefits more from turning off symbolic processing than from spreading one integral across cores.

`InputField[..., Expression]` has quirks with `*^-` entry. A safe workaround is to accept the input as a string, then parse it with `Read[..., Number]`, which understands plain numbers, `E` notation, and `*^` notation.

Example:

```wl
ClearAll[sciInput];
SetAttributes[sciInput, HoldFirst];

inst[numstring_] :=
  InputField[numstring, String, FieldSize -> {20, 1},
   FieldHint -> "enter a number"];

sciInput[result_] :=
  DynamicModule[{sig, nn},
   DynamicWrapper[
    Panel[Row[{inst[Dynamic@sig]}]],
    Quiet[
     If[(nn = Read[StringToStream[sig], Number]] =!= $Failed && NumberQ[nn],
      result = nn
     ]
    ],
    TrackedSymbols :> {sig}
   ]
  ];

sciInput[number] // Deploy
Dynamic[number]
```

This avoids using `Expression` directly and handles scientific notation correctly.

