Use a least-squares fit. If your 6 equations are linear in 3 unknowns, set up the residuals and minimize the sum of their squares.

In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, for variables x, y, z:

NMinimize[
  Total[(lhs - rhs)^2 & /@ {
    eq1, eq2, eq3, eq4, eq5, eq6
  }],
  {x, y, z}
]

Or more compactly, if the equations are of the form a x + b y + c z == d:

NMinimize[
  Total[(Subtract @@@ equations)^2],
  {x, y, z}
]

This returns the “best fit” values of the 3 variables, similar to the resolved example.

Yes. For large 2D coordinate sets, avoid `HistogramList` + `ArrayPlot`; use `BinCounts` directly to build the 2D bins, then convert to an image.

