This is a context-path issue.

After the first load, `Test1`` remains on `$ContextPath`. Then inside `Begin["`Private`"]`, the unqualified name `function1` can resolve to `Test1`function1` instead of `Test1`Private`function1`.

So the second time you run the package, your definition may attach to the public symbol, and both calls print `5`.

Fix it by either:

```mathematica
BeginPackage["Test1`"];
Begin["`Private`"];
Test1`Private`function1[x_] := x
End[];
EndPackage[];
```

or clear the symbol first:

```mathematica
BeginPackage["Test1`"];
Remove[function1];
Begin["`Private`"];
function1[x_] := x
End[];
EndPackage[];
```

If needed, fully reset with `Remove["Test1`*"]` before reloading.

For this kind of problem, brute force over all permutations is fine.

In Mathematica:

```wl
