`Refresh` isn’t the issue by itself — the problem is that `AppendTo[ls, ...]` modifies `ls`, and `Dynamic` is tracking `ls`. So every time `ls` changes, the dynamic updates immediately, which causes another append, and so on.

To get the 10-second behavior, prevent the `Dynamic` from tracking any symbols:

```wl
Dynamic[
  Refresh[
    AppendTo[ls, k++];
    Last@ls,
    UpdateInterval -> 10
  ],
  TrackedSymbols -> {}
]
```

This stops the feedback loop, so `Refresh` can control the update interval properly.

If your data are noisy, first smooth/denoise them, then detect extrema on the cleaned signal.

In Mathematica/Wolfram Language, one common approach is:

1. Denoise with a wavelet transform:
```wl
dwt = DiscreteWaveletTransform[data, SymletWavelet[7], 6];
dwt = WaveletThreshold[dwt];
est = InverseWaveletTransform[dwt];
```

2. Find local minima/maxima from the denoised list:
```wl
findExtremaPos[list_List] := Module[{signs, extremaPos, minPos, maxPos},
  signs = Sign[Differences[list]];
  signs = signs //. {a___, q_, 0, z__} :> {a, q, q, z};
  extremaPos = 1 + Accumulate@(Length /@ Split[signs]);
  If[First@signs == 1,
    {minPos, maxPos} = {extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]], extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]]},
    {minPos, maxPos} = {extremaPos[[1 ;; -2 ;; 2]], extremaPos[[2 ;; -2 ;; 2]]}
  ];
  {minPos, maxPos}
]
```

For newer versions, `FindPeaks` can help for maxima.

For a Lorenz-style “map” of the Rössler system, the same idea applies: define the numerical solution as a function of the parameter(s) you want to vary, then evaluate it inside the animation.

Example pattern:

sol[a_] := {x, y, z} /. 
  NDSolve[
    {x'[t] == -y[t] - z[t],
     y'[t] == x[t] + a y[t],
     z'[t] == b + z[t] (x[t] - c),
     x[0] == 1, y[0] == 0, z[0] == 0},
    {x, y, z}, {t, 0, 100}
  ][[1]]

Animate[
 Module[{f = sol[a]},
  ParametricPlot3D[
   Evaluate[{x[t], y[t], z[t]} /. f],
   {t, 0, 100}
  ]
 ],
 {a, 0.1, 0.5}
]

If you want a true Poincaré/Lorenz map rather than a trajectory animation, you’d compute intersections with a plane and plot successive points.

