Example with explicit positions:

```wl
list = Symbol /@ CharacterRange["a", "n"];

styleRules = {
  {4, 9, 2} -> Red,
  {3, 5, 7} -> Green,
  {8, 1, 6} -> Blue
};
```

Apply them:

```wl
stylePart[expr_, rules_] :=
  ReplacePart[expr,
    rules /. (pos_ -> color_) :> (i : pos :> Style[expr[[i]], color])
  ];

stylePart[list, Flatten[Thread /@ styleRules]]
```

Or use a compact rule form:

```wl
styleRules2 = {
  4 | 9 | 2 -> Red,
  3 | 5 | 7 -> Green,
  8 | 1 | 6 -> Blue
};
```

Then build a `SparseArray` of styles and apply them with `MapThread`. This is usually easier than chaining several `MapAt`s.

A simple way is:

1. Rasterize the 3D object to a grayscale image.
2. Use the brightness as a probability map for random dots.
3. Multiply by an edge mask so the silhouette stays clear.

Example:

```wl
g3 = Graphics3D[{Gray, Sphere[]}, Lighting -> "Neutral", Boxed -> False];

img = ColorConvert[Rasterize[g3, "Image", ImageResolution -> 72], "GrayLevel"];
edge = ColorNegate@EdgeDetect[img];

dots = Image@Map[RandomChoice[{#, 1 - #} -> {1, 0}] &, ImageData@img, {2}];

ImageMultiply[dots, edge]
```

Tuning `ImageAdjust`, `EdgeDetect`, `Dilation`, and upscaling the rasterized image gives more natural “Penrose-style” shading.

If you mean the weather example, you can simulate and plot it with `ListPlot`, for example:

```wolfram
weather = DiscreteMarkovProcess[1, {{0.7, 0.3}, {0.4, 0.6}}];
days = 1000;
data = RandomFunction[weather, {1, days}];

ListPlot[data["Path"][[All, 2]],
  Filling -> Axis,
  Ticks -> {Automatic, {1, 2}},
  PlotRange -> {All, {0.5, 2.5}}
]
```

This plots the state sequence over time, with `1 = rain` and `2 = no rain`.

If you want a nicer labeled plot, you can also replace `1` and `2` with names before plotting.

In Mathematica 10, the usual way to stop `Speak` is to interrupt the kernel (`Alt+.` on Windows/Linux, `Command+.` on Mac). If `Speak` keeps going, you can also check whether it’s still speaking with:

```wl
MathLink`CallFrontEnd[CurrentlySpeakingPacket]
```

A practical workaround is to split the text into short phrases and speak them one by one, so normal interrupting works between phrases:

```wl
Clear[interruptibleSpeak];
interruptibleSpeak[text_String] := Module[{phrases},
  phrases = StringSplit[
    StringReplace[text, (# -> # <> "\n") & /@ {".", ";", "!", "?"}], "\n"
  ];
  Do[
    While[MathLink`CallFrontEnd[CurrentlySpeakingPacket], Pause[0.1]];
    Speak[phrases[[i]]],
    {i, Length@phrases}
  ]
]
```

Then use:

```wl
interruptibleSpeak["your text here"]
```

In Mathematica, define your vectors/matrices as symbolic arrays, then differentiate with respect to the vector:

```mathematica
n = 2;
aMat = Array[a, {n, n}];
xVec = Array[x, n];
bVec = Array[b, n];

f[xVec_] := aMat.xVec - DiagonalMatrix[xVec].(aMat.xVec + bVec);

D[f[xVec], {xVec}]
MatrixForm[%]
```

This returns the Jacobian symbolically.

If you want the result automatically for any `n`, just set `n` first; Mathematica handles the dimensions through `Array` and `DiagonalMatrix`.

Use the *symbol name* as a string, then convert it with `ToExpression` only when you need the symbol.

Example:

```mathematica
syms = {"a", "b", "c", "d"};
vals = {1, 2, -100, 3};
noset = -100;

Remove @@ syms;

MapThread[
  If[#2 != noset, Evaluate[ToExpression[#1]] = #2] &,
  {syms, vals}
];

Solve[a + b == c + d]
```

If you need to find which one has the special value:

```mathematica
syms[[First@First@Position[vals, noset]]]
```

That returns `"c"` in this example.

If you already have the symbols themselves in a list, you can use `MapThread` with the symbol list directly and avoid `Remove`/`Position` tricks.

If you want each list to keep only elements not already seen in earlier lists, use `Complement` with the union of previous lists.

Example:

```mathematica
list1 = {2, 5, 10, 11, 12, 17, 18};
list2 = {5, 8, 11, 16, 19, 21};
list3 = {5, 8, 9, 11, 16, 19, 21, 23};

list1
Complement[list2, list1]
Complement[list3, Union[list1, list2]]
```

This returns:

```mathematica
{2, 5, 10, 11, 12, 17, 18}
{8, 16, 19, 21}
{9, 23}
```

If you have many lists, you can automate this with `FoldList` or a loop over the previous union.

Yes. `Plot` applies a single `RegionFunction` to all curves in the plot, so if each function needs a different region, plot them separately and combine with `Show`.

For your piecewise example, `Piecewise` is easiest:

pw = Piecewise[{
  {2 Sqrt[x], 0 <= x <= 1},
  {4 - 2 x, 1 < x < 2.5},
  {2 x - 7, 2.5 <= x <= 4}
}, Indeterminate];

Plot[pw, {x, -1, 5}]

If you want separate functions/regions:

Show[
 Plot[f1, {x, a1, b1}, RegionFunction -> reg1, PlotStyle -> Red],
 Plot[f2, {x, a2, b2}, RegionFunction -> reg2, PlotStyle -> Blue],
 Plot[f3, {x, a3, b3}, RegionFunction -> reg3, PlotStyle -> Green]
]

For many functions, use `MapThread` + `Show`.

If you want the *most accurate* conversion, don’t resample by hand. Instead, define the transformed coordinates as a new function of the original `InterpolatingFunction`, then build a new interpolation on the original mesh.

For a 1D solution `y[x]`, you can extract its internal grid points and values:

```mathematica
ifun = y;
grid = ifun["Grid"][[1]];
vals = ifun /@ grid;
```

Then transform those points:

```mathematica
newpts = ({Sqrt[#^2 + y[#]^2], ArcTan[y[#], #]} & /@ grid);
newifun = Interpolation[newpts];
```

If you also need derivatives under the coordinate change, use the chain rule. For a transformation
`(x, y) -> (r, θ)`, derivatives must be transformed using the Jacobian matrix, not by interpolating derivatives directly.

If you want, I can show how to compute the transformed derivative explicitly in Mathematica.

Use `EdgeStyle` mapped from the edge weights.

If the graph already exists:

```wl
ew = PropertyValue[g1, EdgeWeight];
el = EdgeList[g1];

edgestyle = Thread[
  el -> (Directive[
      CapForm["Round"],
      Thickness[Rescale[#, {Min[ew], Max[ew]}, {0.02, 0.06}]],
      ColorData["SolarColors"][Rescale[#, {Min[ew], Max[ew]}]]
    ] & /@ ew)
];

g1styled = SetProperty[g1, EdgeStyle -> edgestyle];
```

Or directly when creating it:

```wl
el = {1 <-> 2, 2 <-> 3, 3 <-> 1};
ew = {2, 3, 4};

g = Graph[
  el,
  EdgeWeight -> ew,
  EdgeStyle -> Thread[
    el -> (Directive[
        Thickness[Rescale[#, {Min[ew], Max[ew]}, {0.02, 0.06}]],
        ColorData["SolarColors"][Rescale[#, {Min[ew], Max[ew]}]]
      ] & /@ ew)
  ]
];
```

This makes edge color reflect weight.

Yes. For a non-analytic region, a common approach is to define an indicator function for “inside the region” and integrate over the plane.

Example:

```wl
f[x_, y_] := Boole[y > 0 && y < 1 - x^2/4];

area = NIntegrate[f[x, y], {x, -20, 20}, {y, -20, 20}];
cx = NIntegrate[x f[x, y], {x, -20, 20}, {y, -20, 20}]/area;
cy = NIntegrate[y f[x, y], {x, -20, 20}, {y, -20, 20}]/area;
```

`Boole` is nicer than `If[...]` here.  
For exact results, you can try `Integrate` instead of `NIntegrate`, but it may fail for more complicated regions.

If your region is already a `Region` object, `RegionMeasure` and `RegionCentroid` are also useful.

Use `RegionPlot` for the overlap directly, not the contour plots themselves. The issue is that `ListContourPlot` is just visualizing sampled data, while the common area is defined by the inequalities.

Example:

```wolfram
plot1 = ListContourPlot[
  Flatten[Table[{i, j, Sin[i + j^2]}, {i, 0, 3, 0.1}, {j, 0, 3, 0.1}], 1],
  RegionFunction -> Function[{x, y, z}, z < 1/2]
];

plot2 = ListContourPlot[
  Flatten[Table[{i, j, Cos[i + j^2]}, {i, 0, 3, 0.1}, {j, 0, 3, 0.1}], 1],
  RegionFunction -> Function[{x, y, z}, z < 0.7]
];

plot3 = RegionPlot[
  Sin[i + j^2] < 1/2 && Cos[i + j^2] < 0.7,
  {i, 0, 3}, {j, 0, 3}
];

GraphicsRow@{plot1, plot2, plot3}
```

If you want the area measurement, use `RegionMeasure` on the `RegionPlot` result.

