A fast practical way is to rasterize the polygon and test overlap on the pixel/component level, rather than checking every point geometrically.

Idea:
1. Rasterize the polygon into a binary image.
2. Compare it against your labeled morphological components.
3. Pick the component labels that intersect the polygon’s foreground pixels.

This is efficient and usually much faster than point-in-polygon tests for every pixel.

Example sketch:

```wl
poly = Polygon[{{50, 50}, {50, 250}, {200, 200}}];

polyimage = Binarize @ Graphics[
  poly, PlotRange -> {{0, 300}, {0, 300}}, ImageSize -> 300
];

overlaps = Complement[
  Flatten @ Pick[m, ImageData[polyimage], 0],
  {0}
];
```

Then visualize/check:

```wl
ImageCompose[
  SelectComponents[m, "Label", MemberQ[overlaps, #] &] // Colorize,
  {polyimage, 0.3}
]
```

If you want exact analytic overlap for real-coordinate polygons, that’s a harder geometry problem; rasterization is the usual speed/accuracy tradeoff.

If you have a list of coordinates, convert them to a 2D array first, then plot.

For example, if your points are like `{x, y, value}`:

