A practical way is to “pad, Thread, then remove padding,” while preserving hold and arbitrary heads.

```wl
fred[expr_, head_: List, seq_: All] :=
 Module[{myhold, maxlength, dummy, paddedexpr},
  SetAttributes[myhold, HoldAllComplete];
  maxlength = Max@Cases[expr, head[args___] :> Length@Hold@args, {1}];
  paddedexpr = Replace[expr,
    head[args___] :> RuleCondition@PadRight[myhold@args, maxlength, dummy],
    {1}];
  DeleteCases[Thread[paddedexpr, myhold, seq], dummy, -1] /. myhold -> head
 ]
```

This keeps `Thread`’s full behavior, even for ragged subexpressions, by equalizing lengths temporarily. If you want, I can also show a `MapThread`-based version or optimize this for deeper nesting.

Yes. A common solution is to set a front-end dynamic expression that checks for newly opened notebooks and runs your code:

```wl
i = 0;
SetOptions[$FrontEndSession,
  FrontEndDynamicExpression :>
    Refresh[
      Notebooks[];
      If[GetNewNotebooks[] =!= {}, Print["New one ", ++i]],
      TrackedSymbols :> {}
    ]
]
```

Replace `Print["New one ", ++i]` with whatever code you want to execute for each new notebook.

Note:
- `TrackedSymbols :> {}` is important to avoid recursion/infinite refresh loops.
- This runs when the front end updates, so it’s not truly “on notebook open” at the kernel level, but it works for detecting new notebooks.

