If you want the text size to stay consistent across the whole notebook, use a stylesheet rather than changing individual cells. The resolved thread’s approach is useful here too: before export, temporarily switch each notebook to the stylesheet you want, export, then restore the original style.

Example:

```wl
Do[
  name = Check[NotebookFileName[nb], "NotSaved.nb", NotebookFileName::nosv];
  oldStyle = StyleDefinitions /. Options[nb];
  SetOptions[nb, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]; (* or your custom stylesheet *)
  Export[FileBaseName[name], nb, "PDF"];
  SetOptions[nb, StyleDefinitions -> oldStyle];
, {nb, Notebooks[]}]
```

If the issue is only exported PDF size/margins, adjust the stylesheet or page settings used for export.

The issue is that `Series[..., {t,0,Max[ow]}]` only controls the total power of the dummy variable, not the individual powers of each variable. So terms like `x^2 y^0` and `x^1 y^1` are treated the same if they have the same total weighted degree, but your truncation rule wants separate per-variable cutoffs.

Also, adding `O[x[i]]^(n+1)` after `Normal` does not remove already-generated mixed terms that exceed the desired order in one variable.

A better approach is to rescale variables so the multivariate truncation becomes a single-variable series problem, as in the resolved thread:

weightedMultivariateSeries[f_, vars_List, ord_List] /;
  Length[vars] === Length[ord] && VectorQ[ord, IntegerQ[#] && # > 0 &] :=
 Module[{t, lcm = LCM @@ ord, newf},
  newf = f /. Thread[vars -> vars^(lcm/ord)];
  newf = newf /. Thread[vars -> t*vars];
  Normal[Series[newf, {t, 0, Max@(lcm/ord)}]] /. t -> 1 /. 
   Thread[vars -> vars^(ord/lcm)]
 ]

If you want, I can help adapt this to support `Infinity` too.

