Use a list of rules and build them with Fold/Join, or `ReplaceRepeated` if each stage depends on the previous one.

A compact pattern is:

```mathematica
fn[pars_] := Module[{rules = {externalvars1 -> pars}},
  rules = Join[rules, externalrules /. rules];
  rules = Join[rules, externalfunction[externalvars2 /. rules]];
  externalvars3 /. rules
]
```

If you want a list of delayed rules, use `RuleDelayed` (`:>`) instead of `->`:

```mathematica
rules = {
  externalvars1 :> pars,
  externalrules :> (externalrules /. rules)
};
```

But in most cases, delayed rules are not the best fit here; building the rule list step by step is clearer and safer. If you want, I can show how to generalize this to an arbitrary number of rule blocks.

Yes. Use a fixed list and overwrite elements in a rotating position instead of rebuilding the list each time.

Example:

big = Range@1*^7;
size = Length@big;
pointer = size;

updateElement[new_Integer] := (
  pointer = 1 + Mod[pointer, size];
  big[[pointer]] = new
)

Do[updateElement[RandomInteger@99], {100}] // AbsoluteTiming

This is much faster than:

big = Append[Rest@big, RandomInteger@99]

If you need the list in “normal” order afterward, rotate it once:

big = RotateLeft[big, Mod[pointer, size]];
pointer = size;

This gives you an efficient circular buffer.

