You can make the front-end behave more like a terminal by creating custom evaluatable cells and giving them your own evaluator.

The key is CellEvaluationFunction. For example, this creates a cell that sends its contents to the system shell:

evaluatableCell[label_String, evaluationFunction_] :=
  (CellPrint[
     TextCell["", "Program",
      Evaluatable -> True,
      CellEvaluationFunction -> evaluationFunction,
      CellFrameLabels -> {{None, label}, {None, None}},
      CellGroupingRules -> "InputGrouping"]
   ];
   SelectionMove[EvaluationNotebook[], All, EvaluationCell];
   NotebookDelete[];
   SelectionMove[EvaluationNotebook[], Next, CellContents];
  )

Then:

evaluatableCell["shell", Import["!" ~~ #, "Text"] &]

This prints a shell-like cell; when you evaluate it, the input is replaced by a fresh evaluatable cell. If you want to keep the original text, remove the SelectionMove/NotebookDelete lines.

You can get pretty close in Mathematica by customizing notebook cells with `CellEvaluationFunction`.

The trick is to make cells that evaluate to something else, like the system shell:

```wl
evaluatableCell[label_String, evaluationFunction_] :=
  (CellPrint[
     TextCell["", "Program",
      Evaluatable -> True,
      CellEvaluationFunction -> evaluationFunction,
      CellFrameLabels -> {{None, label}, {None, None}},
      CellGroupingRules -> "InputGrouping"]
   ];
   SelectionMove[EvaluationNotebook[], All, EvaluationCell];
   NotebookDelete[];
   SelectionMove[EvaluationNotebook[], Next, CellContents];
  )

evaluatableCell["shell", Import["!"~~#, "Text"]&]
```

This creates a notebook-style input area where evaluation runs shell commands. If you want a less destructive workflow, remove the `SelectionMove`/`NotebookDelete` lines.

So yes: Mathematica can be adapted as a notebook-like terminal, though it’s not a full terminal emulator.

Yes — if you want the roots ordered by argument (angle) around the unit circle, sort them with `Arg`/`ArcTan` before plotting.

Example in Mathematica:

```mathematica
ClearAll[z];
b = (z (z - 0.5) (z + 0.5))/((1 - 0.5 z) (1 + 0.5 z));

sol = z /. NSolve[b == 1, z];
data = {Re[#], Im[#]} & /@ sol;

sorted = SortBy[data, ArcTan @@ # &];

Graphics[
 {
  Circle[{0, 0}, 1],
  Red, PointSize[0.03], Point[sorted],
  Line[Join[sorted, {First[sorted]}]]
 },
 Axes -> True
]
```

