If `parameters` is a small set of optional options, the Wolfram Language convention is to use `OptionsPattern[]` and `OptionValue`:

```wl
Options[myFunction] = {param1 -> 1, param2 -> 2};

myFunction[variable_, opts : OptionsPattern[]] := Module[{p1, p2},
  p1 = OptionValue[param1];
  p2 = OptionValue[param2];
  (* body *)
]
```

This is clearer and faster than repeated `/.` substitutions.

If you already have a rule list, convert once at the top:

```wl
myFunction[variable_, parameters_] := Module[{p1, p2},
  {p1, p2} = {param1, param2} /. parameters;
  (* body *)
]
```

For `Compile`, rule substitution and symbolic option handling are generally not compatible. Best is to pass plain numeric arguments directly to the compiled function, not rules or formulas.

Use `:>` (`RuleDelayed`) instead of `->` (`Rule`).

`->` evaluates the right-hand side immediately, before `amp` and `afreq` are substituted. `:>` delays that evaluation until after pattern variables are bound.

Example:

```wl
s = {10 Cos[-Pi t], -4 Sin[0.23 t]};

f2[a1_, a2_, a3_] := Module[{a = a1, phi = a3},
  Function[a a2[phi #1]]
];

f1 := Function[f2[#1, #2, #3]];

l = Cases[s, amp_ trig_[afreq_ t] :> f1[amp, trig, afreq]];
l[[1]][.33]
```

This gives the numeric result you expect.

In short: for nested functions/patterns, prefer `:>` when the RHS depends on matched variables.

If the plots are already saved as graphics expressions, just combine them with Show:

Show[plot1, plot2]

Example:

p1 = Plot[Sin[x], {x, 0, 2 Pi}];
p2 = Plot[Cos[x], {x, 0, 2 Pi}];
Show[p1, p2]

To change line colors, specify PlotStyle when creating each plot:

p1 = Plot[Sin[x], {x, 0, 2 Pi}, PlotStyle -> Red];
p2 = Plot[Cos[x], {x, 0, 2 Pi}, PlotStyle -> Blue];
Show[p1, p2]

If the plots were created in different notebooks, assign each plot to a variable in its notebook, then use those variables together in Show.

If you already have separate plots, combining them with GraphicsGrid is a standard approach. To make it easier and keep all panels on the same scale, compute a common PlotRange and reuse it for each plot.

A compact pattern is:

```mathematica
sel[x_] := Select[t, #[[1]] == "Reg" <> ToString[x] &];
plots = ListLinePlot[sel[#][[All, 2 ;; 3]], PlotRange -> All] & /@ Range[3];
ranges = PlotRange /. (Options[#, PlotRange] & /@ plots);
pr = {{Min[ranges[[All, 1, 1]]], Max[ranges[[All, 1, 2]]]},
      {Min[ranges[[All, 2, 1]]], Max[ranges[[All, 2, 2]]]}};

GraphicsGrid[
  {ListLinePlot[sel[#][[All, 2 ;; 3]], PlotRange -> pr] & /@ Range[3]},
  Dividers -> All
]
```

If you want a more automatic “trellis” workflow, use `ListPlot`/`ListLinePlot` with `GroupedBy` or `ParallelTable` depending on your data structure.

Use `Show` to combine multiple `Plot`s, one per parameter value, and generate the curves from a list of values.

Example:

```wl
vals = {1, 3, 5};

plots = Table[
  Module[{sol = First @ Solve[a x + y == 7 && x - y == 1, {x, y}]},
    Plot[Evaluate[(y + x)/(x + r) /. sol], {r, 0, 3}, PlotRange -> All]
  ],
  {a, vals}
];

Show[plots, PlotRange -> All, PlotLegends -> Placed[vals, Right]]
```

If your real problem involves `NDSolve`, the same idea applies: define a function that returns the solution for each parameter, then `Table` over the parameter list and `Show` the resulting plots.

