A better approach is to let Mathematica measure and wrap the text for you, rather than guessing the height from `StringLength`.

Use `TextCell` inside `Framed`, then place that in `Inset` with a fixed width. `TextCell` will wrap automatically:

```wl
wrappedFrame[txt_, w_: 100] :=
 Graphics[{
   Inset[
    Framed[
     TextCell[
      Style[txt, LineIndent -> 0, TextAlignment -> Left],
      "Text",
      CellMargins -> {{10, 10}, {10, 10}}
     ]
    ],
    {0, 0}, {0, 0}, {w, Automatic}
   ]
 }]
```

If you want a more “layout-like” solution, `Pane` is also good:

```wl
Graphics[{
  Inset[
   Framed[
    Pane[Style[txt, TextAlignment -> Left], w]
   ]
  ]
}]
```

So: don’t estimate height; use `TextCell` or `Pane` and let the front end compute the wrapped size.

Yes — for PlotLegends specifically, the legend is a FrontEnd box object, so the best way is usually to set a global box option rather than styling each legend manually.

For example, to set the default font family for graphics/plot labels and legends:

```wl
SetOptions[$FrontEndSession,
  GraphicsBoxOptions -> {
    BaseStyle -> {FontFamily -> "Arial"}
  }
]
```

If you want it to persist across sessions, use:

```wl
SetOptions[$FrontEnd,
  GraphicsBoxOptions -> {
    BaseStyle -> {FontFamily -> "Arial"}
  }
]
```

If you only want legends, you can still use `Legending`/`LegendContainer`, but for global graphics text styling, `GraphicsBoxOptions` is the more general solution.

Yes — the basic approach in Mathematica is:

1. Convert the text to outline graphics with `Text` + `Graphics`.
2. Extract the polygon/curve coordinates for each glyph.
3. Map those coordinates onto a parametric curve using arc length.

A practical shortcut is to treat each character as a graphic primitive and position/rotate it along the curve.

Example sketch for a “rainbow” style along `Sin[x]`:

```wl
text = "The quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog";
chars = Characters[text];
xpos = Range[0, StringLength[text]-1]*0.4;

curve[x_] := {x, Sin[x]};
tangent[x_] := Normalize[{1, Cos[x]}];

Graphics[
 Table[
  Module[{p = curve[x], ang = ArcTan @@ tangent[x]},
   Inset[
    Rotate[Style[chars[[i]], 18], ang + Pi/2],
    p
   ]
  ],
  {i, Length[chars]}, {x, {xpos[[i]]}}
 ],
 PlotRange -> All
]
```

This places each character on the curve and rotates it to follow the tangent. For true baseline-fit text, you’d need glyph outlines and more geometry work.

Yes. In Mathematica, `Function` holds its arguments, so you need `Evaluate` to splice in the variable list:

```wl
variables = {a, b};
values = {1, 2};

f = Function[Evaluate @ variables, 2 a + b];
f @@ values
(* 4 *)
```

If the symbols may already have values assigned, it’s safer to keep them in a held form:

```wl
variables = Hold[a, b];
values = {1, 2};

f = variables /. _[vars__] :> Function[{vars}, 2 a + b];
f @@ values
(* 4 *)
```

So yes, you can create a function with a variable number of named arguments from a list.

If your function has 4 varying arguments, a common approach in Mathematica is to fix one variable with `Manipulate` and visualize the remaining 3D slice.

For example, for `f[a,b,c,d]`, you can do:

```mathematica
Manipulate[
 ContourPlot3D[
  f[x, y, z, w],
  {x, 0, 1}, {y, 0, 1}, {z, 0, 1}
 ],
 {w, 0, 1}
]
```

This lets you explore how the 3D shape changes as `w` varies.

If you want another view, use `SliceContourPlot3D` or create several fixed-parameter `Plot3D`/`ContourPlot3D` plots. For 4D data, interactive slicing is usually the most practical option.

