If you want a tangent that “moves” along the plotted curve, first interpolate the data, then use the derivative of that interpolation to get the slope at any x.

Example:

data = {{5 10^-3, 0}, {4.65 10^-3, 100}, {4.32 10^-3, 200},
  {3.74 10^-3, 400}, {3.23 10^-3, 600}, {2.6 10^-3, 900},
  {2.09 10^-3, 1200}, {1.68 10^-3, 1500}};

f = Interpolation[data];
df = f';

x0 = .003;
y0 = f[x0];
dir = Normalize[{1, df[x0]}];

ListLinePlot[data, Epilog -> Line[{ {x0, y0} - 1000 dir, {x0, y0} + 1000 dir }]]

Change x0 to move the tangent to a different point on the curve. If you want, I can also show how to make x0 interactive with Manipulate[].

You’re not doing anything “wrong” mathematically, but `Solve` won’t use your abstract `Assumptions` the way you expect for matrix objects.

In Mathematica, `Element[X, Vectors[n]]` does not make `X` behave like a symbolic vector with components. To solve elementwise, define explicit arrays, e.g. for `n = 3`:

```mathematica
xvec = Array[x, n];
zvec = Array[z, n];
ymat = Array[y, {n, n}];
fumat = DiagonalMatrix[Array[fu, n]];

Solve[ymat.fumat.(zvec + xvec.ymat) + (zvec + xvec.ymat).fumat.ymat + 2 lam*xvec == 0, xvec]
```

This gives a symbolic solution for the entries of `xvec`.

Also, `ExpandAll`/`Simplify` won’t automatically expand matrix equations under those assumptions. If you want, I can help rewrite your exact equation in component form.

You can do it by generating the controls from the remaining indices, and keeping the control constructor `HoldFirst`-aware.

A compact version is:

```wl
DynamicModule[{fC = 1, sC = 2, c},
  allC = {{1, 2}, {10, 20}, {100, 200}, {1000, 2000}};
  lC = Range[Length[allC]];

  Column[{
    Dynamic@SetterBar[Dynamic@fC, lC],
    Dynamic@SetterBar[Dynamic@sC, Complement[lC, {fC}]],

    Dynamic@Table[
      With[{i = i},
        Slider[Dynamic@c[i], allC[[i]]]
      ],
      {i, Complement[lC, {fC, sC}]}
    ]
  }]
]
```

If you want a reusable generator, define it with `HoldFirst`:

```wl
SetAttributes[controlGenerate, HoldFirst];
controlGenerate[var_, range_] := Slider[var, range];
```

Then use `Dynamic@controlGenerate[...]` for each dynamically created control.

