Yes — if by “fill in” you mean find where a closed sampled curve intersects a plane, the standard approach is to detect sign changes in the plane equation along the ordered points, then interpolate between the two bracketing points.

For a plane `z == 0.5`:

```wl
pts = dt;
vals = pts[[All, 3]] - 0.5;

zc = zeroCrossings[vals];

intersections = Table[
  With[{p1 = pts[[i]], p2 = pts[[j]], v1 = vals[[i]], v2 = vals[[j]]},
    p1 + (0 - v1) (p2 - p1)/(v2 - v1)
  ],
  {{i, j}, zc}
]
```

This returns the approximate intersection points.

If you want a closed curve “filled in” as a region, that’s a different task: you’d typically project it to a plane and use `Region`/`BoundaryMeshRegion`/`Polygon` to construct an interior.

