The order of the eigenvectors is not fixed. `Eigenvectors` may return them in any order, and the order can change if the matrix is perturbed or if you use higher precision.

If you need the vectors in a specific order, sort them yourself, for example by their associated eigenvalues:

```mathematica
{vals, vecs} = Eigensystem[M];
ord = Ordering[vals];
vecs = vecs[[ord]];
```

Then build `U = Transpose[vecs]`.

If your concern is orthonormal accuracy, machine-precision input limits you to about 10^-16. For better accuracy, use higher-precision input first:

```mathematica
{vals, vecs} = Eigensystem[SetPrecision[M, 32]];
U = Transpose[Normalize /@ vecs];
```

So: order is arbitrary unless you sort it, and higher orthonormal accuracy requires higher-precision data.

`Eigensystem` does not promise a mathematically “canonical” order. It returns eigenvalues/eigenvectors in the order most convenient for the underlying algorithm. For general matrices, eigenvalues may be complex, and there is no natural total order on complex numbers.

For a real symmetric matrix, if you want the usual ascending order, just sort them yourself:

```mathematica
{vals, vecs} = Eigensystem[m];
ord = Ordering[vals];
{vals, vecs} = {vals[[ord]], vecs[[ord]]};
```

If you want ordering by magnitude, use `Ordering[Abs[vals]]`.

`Timing` inside `Manipulate` won’t give a stable performance plot, because `Manipulate` itself adds overhead and often reuses cached results. Also, `Timing` measures only the evaluation at that moment, not the total cost of the whole interactive update.

A better approach is to measure outside `Manipulate`, for each `i`, and then graph the results:

```mathematica
times = Table[
  First @ Timing[Show[somegraphs[[1 ;; i]]]],
  {i, 1, 500}
];

ListLinePlot[times, AxesLabel -> {"i", "time (s)"}]
```

If you want more reliable timings, use `RepeatedTiming` instead of `Timing`:

```mathematica
times = Table[
  First @ RepeatedTiming[Show[somegraphs[[1 ;; i]]]],
  {i, 1, 500}
];
```

This will show how runtime changes as `i` increases.

If you want to read a CSV in batches and strip comment lines, you can stream the file and filter as you go.

Example in Wolfram Language:

```wl
Clear[readRows];
readRows[stream_, n_] :=
  Module[{str = ReadList[stream, "String", n]},
    If[str === {}, $Failed,
      ImportString[
        StringJoin@Riffle[
          Select[str, Not@StringStartsQ[#, "#"] &],
          "\n"
        ],
        "Table"
      ]
    ]
  ];
```

Usage:

```wl
str = OpenRead["file.csv"];
While[readRows[str, 1000] =!= $Failed, Null];
Close[str];
```

