You can avoid generating the full `Tuples` grid by iterating only around a “center line” and testing nearby integer points.

For example:

```mathematica
lin[x_] := (x[[2]] > (1.9*x[[1]] - 4)) && (x[[2]] < (1.9*x[[1]] + 3));

line = Table[{i, 1.9 i + 0.5}, {i, -35, 35}];

points = Flatten[
  DeleteCases[
    Table[
      If[lin[Round[line[[i]] + {0, j}]], Round[line[[i]] + {0, j}], Null],
      {i, 1, Length[line]}, {j, -50, 50}
    ],
    Null, 2
  ],
  1
];
```

This is much better than building all lattice points in a box first.  
If your inequalities are more general, the same idea still applies: estimate the relevant region, then test only points near it.

Use the previous root as the initial guess for the next `FindRoot` call, and store each solution by `n`.

A simple pattern is:

```mathematica
sol[60] = x /. FindRoot[MySum[60], {x, x0}, WorkingPrecision -> 7];

Do[
  sol[n] = x /. FindRoot[MySum[n], {x, sol[n - 5]}, WorkingPrecision -> 7],
  {n, 65, 300, 5}
]
```

If you want to increment by 1 instead:

```mathematica
sol[60] = x /. FindRoot[MySum[60], {x, x0}];
Do[
  sol[n] = x /. FindRoot[MySum[n], {x, sol[n - 1]}],
  {n, 61, 300}
]
```

This is the same idea as the resolved thread: each new run starts from the root found in the previous step, helping `FindRoot` stay on the branch you want.

Yes. The usual trick is to draw the axes separately, then overlay them on the plot.

Example:

```wl
hsgm = Histogram[RandomVariate[NormalDistribution[0, 1], 1000]];

axes = Plot[I, {x, 0, 1},
  AxesOrigin -> {-4, -10},
  PlotRange -> {{-3, 3}, {0, 200}}
] // FullGraphics;

Show[
  {hsgm /. (Axes -> _) :> (Axes -> False), axes},
  PlotRange -> All
]
```

This lets you “break” the axes where you want instead of having them span the whole range.

If you also want labels like `FrameLabel`, you generally need to add them manually, since using `Frame` would remove the disconnected-axis look.

To plot an ellipse in Mathematica, use `EllipsoidQuantile` (for a data-based ellipse) or `Ellipse`/`ParametricPlot` for a geometric one.

For your case, the resolved approach is:

```wl
Needs["MultivariateStatistics`"]

cutscore = 0.5;
data1 = RandomVariate[
  MultinormalDistribution[{0, 0}, {{1, 0.6}, {0.6, 1}}],
  500
];

ellipse1 = EllipsoidQuantile[data1, 0.5];
e1 = Graphics[ellipse1];

Show[
  e1,
  Graphics[{
    LightBlue,
    Polygon[
      DeleteCases[
        Cases[e1, Line[pts_] :> pts, Infinity][[1]],
        {_?(# < cutscore &), _}
      ]
    ]
  }],
  Axes -> True
]
```

If you want, I can also show a simpler example for drawing a plain ellipse from scratch.

Use the difference directly in ColorFunction, and turn off automatic scaling.

Example:

f1[x_] := Sin[x];
f2[x_] := Cos[x];

Plot[{f1[x], f2[x]}, {x, 0, 2 Pi},
 ColorFunction -> Function[{x, y},
   ColorData[{"Rainbow", {-1.5, 1.5}}][f2[x] - f1[x]]
 ],
 ColorFunctionScaling -> False,
 Filling -> {2 -> {1}}
]

Notes:
- ColorFunction gets the absolute x, y coordinates, but you can ignore y and compute f2[x] - f1[x] yourself.
- Use ColorFunctionScaling -> False so Mathematica does not rescale the value.
- Adjust {-1.5, 1.5} to match the expected min/max difference.

For ListLinePlot, use Interpolation on the data if needed.

Use `Graphics` with a `Circle`, then add the colored lines and point as separate primitives. For example:

