Yes. Define a numerical function that runs NDSolve for each A and returns a single scalar, then plot that function versus A.

Example pattern:

f[a_?NumericQ] := Module[{sol},
  sol = NDSolve[
    {
      y''[t] + 500 y'[t] + 100 y[t] == -a (Cos[500 t] + 2 Cos[1000 t]),
      y[0] == 0, y'[0] == 0,
      300 x'[t] == 1000 y[t] + 500 y'[t] - 35 Tanh[50 x'[t]],
      x[0] == 0
    },
    {x, y}, {t, 0, 0.1}
  ];
  (x[0.1]/0.1) /. sol[[1]]
]

Plot[f[a], {a, 0, 100}]

If you want average velocity over the interval, use x[tend]/tend (or better, (x[t2]-x[t1])/(t2-t1)). `NumericQ` prevents symbolic evaluation during Plot.

Use explicit x-coordinates instead of relying on equally spaced columns.

For your segments, take the midpoint of each segment as the x-position:

pos = Mean /@ segments;

Then build data as {x, y, z} points and plot those:

newdata = Flatten[
   Table[{pos[[i]], j, data[[j, i]]}, {j, Length[data]}, {i, Length[pos]}],
   1
];

ListContourPlot[newdata,
  AspectRatio -> 1/10,
  ImageSize -> 700,
  FrameTicks -> {{Automatic, None},
    {Union @@ segments, None}}
]

If you want labels like “Seg.1”, “Seg.2”, etc., use custom tick labels with the same positions. Adding boundary points at x = 0 and x = 40 can help enforce the full range.

For multiple graphs in one picture, use Graphics/Show rather than Contour3DPlot.

