If you want 2D curves to appear “thicker” in a 3D plot, a simple approach is to turn each curve into a filled ribbon made of two offset curves.

Example:

data = {#, Sin[#], #^2} & /@ Range[0, 10, 1/4];

addThickness[data_, f_] :=
  Transpose[
    data /. {x_, y_, z_} :> {{x, y}, {x, y - f z}, {x, y + f z}}
  ];

ListLinePlot[
  addThickness[data, 0.01],
  Filling -> {2 -> {1}, 3 -> {1}},
  PlotStyle -> Blue,
  FillingStyle -> Opacity[1]
]

This uses Filling to simulate thickness. If you want, I can also show how to do this with multiple curves or in actual 3D graphics.

For discrete integer points, `ContourPlot3D` isn’t the best tool. Use the integer lattice plus `Select` to keep only points satisfying your condition.

Example:

With[{max = 5},
 Module[{pts, good},
  pts = Tuples[Range[1, max], 3];
  good = Select[pts, #[[1]] - 2 #[[2]] - #[[3]] == 0 && #[[1]] != #[[2]] &];
  Graphics3D[{
    Gray, Sphere[pts, 0.08],
    Red, Sphere[good, 0.12]
  }, BoxRatios -> {1, 1, 1}]
 ]
]

