`NDSolve` can choke on vector-valued equations written in a compact matrix form, especially with constant terms like `0. {{0}, {0}, {1.}}`.

A reliable workaround is to write the system explicitly and use `Thread`:

```mathematica
X[t_] = {X1[t], X2[t], X3[t]};
A = {{0, 1., 0.}, {0, 0., 1.}, {0., -0.156816, -0.523512}};
B = {0, 0, 0};

sol = NDSolve[
  {Thread[X'[t] == A.X[t] + B],
   Thread[X[0] == {0, 0, 0}]},
  X[t], {t, 0, 1}
]
```

If the constant term is meant to be nonzero, replace `B` accordingly.

If you want, I can also show the `ParametricNDSolve` version.

Yes — if your “vector function” is already given by NDSolve, you can usually feed that numerical solution into another NDSolve, but not as a symbolic expression.

Typical pattern:

```wl
sol1 = NDSolve[{eq1, ic1}, {u, v}, {t, 0, 10}];
uFun[t_] := u[t] /. First[sol1];
vFun[t_] := v[t] /. First[sol1];

sol2 = NDSolve[{eq2 /. {u -> uFun, v -> vFun}, ic2}, x, {t, 0, 10}];
```

If your equation depends on a vector-valued solution from the first solve, define it as an interpolating function and substitute that numeric function into the second system.

Key point: NDSolve returns numerical interpolating functions, so use them as functions, not as symbolic formulas. If you want, paste your equations and I can show the exact syntax.

If you want the points on the circle colored by local density, use a kernel density estimate and map that density back to each point’s color.

2D version:

```wl
points = Map[Normalize, RandomReal[{-1, 1}, {3000, 2}]];

d = SmoothKernelDistribution[points];
colors = Hue /@ Rescale[PDF[d, #] & /@ points];

Graphics[Transpose[{colors, Point /@ points}]]
```

If you want the density only along the circumference, convert points to angles first:

```wl
arc = ArcTan @@@ points;
d = SmoothKernelDistribution[arc];
colors = Hue /@ Rescale[PDF[d, #] & /@ arc];

ListPlot[Map[List, points], PlotStyle -> colors, AspectRatio -> Automatic]
