The slowdown is likely because the interpolating functions are not actually available on the subkernels, so they get serialized/copied inefficiently or re-evaluated.

Fix: define the interpolation objects on each parallel kernel before running the parallel computation, e.g.

ParallelEvaluate[
  ip = Interpolation[...];
  dataMod[] := Module[{}, ... ip[t] ...]
];

Then run:

ParallelEvaluate[dataMod[]]

If the interpolations are large, this is much faster than trying to send them with every call. Also, avoid globals like X, Y, Z inside Module; make them local with `x`, `y`, `z` or `Module[{X,Y,Z,...}, ...]`.

In Mathematica, align both series by the union of dates, then carry the last known price forward for missing dates.

A simple approach:

```wl
fillForCompare[data1_, data2_] :=
 Module[{f1, f2, all, last},
  all = Union @@ {data1, data2}[[All, All, 1]];
  (f1[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data1;
  (f2[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data2;
  f1[_] := last; f2[_] := last;
  {all, f1 /@ all, f2 /@ all}\[Transpose]
 ]
```

This returns rows like:

```wl
{{date, price1, price2}, ...}
```

If you want separate filled lists:

```wl
fillForCompare2[data1_, data2_] :=
 Module[{f1, f2, all, last},
  all = Union @@ {data1, data2}[[All, All, 1]];
  (f1[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data1;
  (f2[#] := last = #2) & @@@ data2;
  f1[_] := last; f2[_] := last;
  {Transpose[{all, f1 /@ all}], Transpose[{all, f2 /@ all}]}
 ]
```

