In Mathematica, use the continuous-time Butterworth model, convert it to discrete time, then filter the list with `RecurrenceFilter`.

Example:

```mathematica
data1 = Table[Exp[-i^2], {i, -5, 5, .01}];
noise = RandomReal[{-0.3, 1}, Length[data1]];
data2 = data1 + noise;

filtered = RecurrenceFilter[
  ToDiscreteTimeModel[ButterworthFilterModel[5], 1],
  data2
];
```

If you want a different cutoff/sample rate, specify the model parameters accordingly before converting to discrete time.

So the key is: `ButterworthFilterModel[...]` for the filter, `ToDiscreteTimeModel[..., 1]` to discretize, and `RecurrenceFilter` to apply it to your vector data.

You can do this much faster by avoiding `Scan`/`Sow` inside nested loops and instead filtering each point list with a compiled function.

A compact version of the accepted solution is:

```wl
getDistantPoints =
  Compile[{{pts, _Real, 2}, {delta, _Real}},
    Module[{res = Table[{0., 0.}, {Length[pts]}], ctr = 1},
      res[[1]] = pts[[1]];
      Do[
        If[Norm[pts[[i]] - res[[ctr]]] > delta,
          res[[++ctr]] = pts[[i]]
        ],
        {i, Length[pts]}
      ];
      Take[res, ctr]
    ],
    CompilationTarget -> "C",
    RuntimeOptions -> "Speed"
  ];
```

Then apply it to your data:

```wl
GZFastAlt[delta_, data_] :=
  ParallelTable[
    Table[
      getDistantPoints[data[[subNO, dispNo, All, ;; 2]], delta],
      {dispNo, Length[data[[subNO]]]}
    ],
    {subNO, 5}
  ];
```

This keeps the data packed and is much more efficient for large datasets.

You can do this by cycling through the function list and mapping elementwise:

functionMap[funcs_List, data_List] := Module[{fn = RotateRight[funcs]},
  First[(fn = RotateLeft[fn])][#] & /@ data
]

Example:

f1[a_] := -a;
f2[a_] := 100 a;
f3[a_] := a/10.0;

functionMap[{f1, f2, f3}, Range[20]]

This applies f1, f2, f3, then repeats for the next elements.

