To center zero frequency, don’t rotate the frequency axis only — shift both the data and the spectrum consistently.

In Wolfram Language, the usual approach is:

```wl
fft = Fourier[list, FourierParameters -> {-1, 1}];
fftShifted = FourierShift[fft];
freqs = FourierFrequencyRange[Length[list], dt];
freqsShifted = RotateLeft[freqs, Length[freqs]/2];
```

Or, if you want the common FFT-style centering:

```wl
fft = Fourier[list, FourierParameters -> {-1, 1}];
fftCentered = RotateRight[fft, Length[list]/2];
freqCentered = RotateRight[Range[-n/2, n/2 - 1]/(n dt), n/2];
```

Also make sure the x-values in `list` are uniformly spaced and that `dt` matches the actual sample spacing. A mismatch there often causes the “weird” zero-frequency artifact.

