Yes. If the matrix is diagonally dominant, a good sparse approximate inverse can be built iteratively from a sparse diagonal preconditioner.

Idea:
1. Start with the inverse of the diagonal.
2. Compute residual: R = I - A.InverseApprox
3. Update: InverseApprox = InverseApprox + InverseApprox.R

In Mathematica terms, a simple approach is:

inv1 = SparseArray[Band[{1, 1}] -> 1/Normal[Diagonal[s]], {n, n}, 0.];
iden = SparseArray[Band[{1, 1}] -> 1., {n, n}, 0.];
res1 = inv1.s - iden;
delta1 = -res1.inv1;
inv2 = inv1 + delta1;

Repeat as needed.

This keeps things sparse much better than trying to form `LinearSolve[s][IdentityMatrix[n]]` directly.

