Yes—use a symbolic/numeric CAS rather than doing Gram–Schmidt by hand.

For Hermite functions on [-1,1], the recurrence is:
u[1] = HermiteH[1, x];
Do[
  u[n] = HermiteH[n, x] - Sum[Integrate[HermiteH[n, x] u[i], {x, -1.0, 1.0}], {i, 1, n - 1}],
  {n, 2, 45}
];

This generates the orthogonalized terms up to at least the 45th, so you can extract the 40th, 41st, and 42nd terms directly.

If you want, I can also show how to normalize them, or rewrite this for Python/SymPy or MATLAB.

The issue is that `Function[...]` is not the main problem; the bigger issue is that `u[[j]]` depends on previously computed `u` terms, and your loop structure/definitions make Mathematica keep symbolic `Integrate` expressions around in a way that can recurse badly.

A direct version is:

inn = Integrate[#1*#2, {x, -Pi, Pi}] &;
v = x^Range[0, 5];
u = ConstantArray[0, Length@v];
u[[1]] = v[[1]];

Do[
  u[[i]] = v[[i]] - Total@Table[
      u[[j]]*inn[v[[i]], u[[j]]]/inn[u[[j]], u[[j]]],
      {j, 1, i - 1}
    ],
  {i, 2, Length@v}
]

Then `u` is an orthogonal basis.

If you want orthonormal, normalize each vector:
`u/ Sqrt[inn[#, #] & /@ u]` is not quite right directly; better compute each norm separately.

Also, `Orthogonalize[x^Range[5], Integrate[#1*#2, {x, -Pi, Pi}] &]` does this automatically.

