Yes, but for a general 6-node triangular prism the closed form becomes extremely large.

In Mathematica you can set it up like this:

```mathematica
n = 1/2 {
  (1 - zeta) (1 - xi - eta),
  xi (1 - zeta),
  eta (1 - zeta),
  (1 + zeta) (1 - xi - eta),
  xi (1 + zeta),
  eta (1 + zeta)
};

xe = {x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, x6};
ye = {y1, y2, y3, y4, y5, y6};
ze = {z1, z2, z3, z4, z5, z6};

x = n.xe; y = n.ye; z = n.ze;
J = D[{x, y, z}, {{xi, eta, zeta}, 1}] // Transpose;

s[i_] := {D[n[[i]], xi], D[n[[i]], eta], D[n[[i]], zeta]};
k[i_, j_] := Simplify[s[i].Inverse[J.J\[Transpose]].s[j] Det[J]];
```

But `k[i,j]` expands to a very long rational expression; for the full matrix, symbolic integration is usually impractical.

If you need a usable result, common alternatives are:
- assume a simpler prism geometry,
- use numerical quadrature,
- or derive the stiffness matrix in a symbolic form with specific node coordinates.

`Return` only exits the nearest enclosing construct. In your code, that is `Do`, not `Module`, so `Print["test"]` still runs.

Use:

```mathematica
MyMod := Module[{i},
  Do[
    Return[1, Module],
    {i, 3}
  ];
  Print["test"]
]
```

Then `MyMod` evaluates to `1`.

So the behavior hasn’t changed; `Return` needs the second argument to specify the enclosing head to return from.

Yes — with graph/graphics you can do this by introducing an intermediate vertex at the midpoint of the edge, then reconnecting edges through it.

A simple pattern is:

1. Add a new vertex.
2. Add edges from the original endpoints to that vertex, plus to your enzyme node.
3. Delete the original edge.
4. Optionally set coordinates manually so the new vertex sits at the midpoint.

Example:

```wl
g = Graph[{"S", "P", "E"}, {"S" \[UndirectedEdge] "P"},
  VertexLabels -> "Name", ImagePadding -> 25];

g1 = VertexAdd[g, "NewNode"];

g2 = EdgeAdd[g1, {
  "S" \[UndirectedEdge] "NewNode",
  "NewNode" \[UndirectedEdge] "P",
  "NewNode" \[UndirectedEdge] "E"
}];

g3 = EdgeDelete[g2, {"S" \[UndirectedEdge] "P"}];

g4 = SetProperty[g3, VertexCoordinates -> {{0, 2}, {2, 2}, {1, 1}, {1, 2}}]
```

This is the standard way to “connect to the middle of an edge” in Mathematica. If you want, I can also show how to automate this for many enzyme-substrate-product cases.

`Piecewise[envpart]` is fine, but inside `Module` you should return the function itself, not a sampled value.

Try:

```wl
getinter[a_, b_, u0_, k_, m_, hbar_, Nu_, Np_, up_] := 
 Module[{ekp, ms, LUs, env, eenv, envpart, f},
  ekp = energies[a, b, u0, k, 1, m, hbar, 0.001, 10^-15][[1]];
  ms = effmass[a, b, u0, k, 0.2, 1, m, hbar, 0.001, 10^-15];
  LUs = BuildLUs[a, b, Nu, Np, u0, up];
  env[x_] := Abs[Det[envfunc[ms, hbar, x, up, Nu, Np, a, b]]]^2;
  eenv = zeros[env, up + 0.000001, 0.01, 0, 1, 10^-15, 0.02][[1]];
  envpart = getpsipieces[LUs[[1]], LUs[[2]], eenv, ms, hbar];
  f = Piecewise[envpart] &;
  f
 ]
```

Then use:

```wl
f = getinter[...];
f[0.231]
```

