If your integral is defined numerically, `D` won’t automatically “see through” `NIntegrate` unless you help it.

Use a numeric definition for the integral, then separately define its derivative:

```wl
ClearAll[T, dT];

T[t_?NumericQ] := NIntegrate[Exp[(eta - 1) t], {eta, 0, 4}];

Block[{NIntegrate, NumericQ = (True &)},
  SetAttributes[NIntegrate, HoldAll];
  dT[t_?NumericQ] = D[T[t], t];
];

T /: D[T[t_], t_] := dT[t];

Plot[Evaluate[D[T[t], t]], {t, 0, 1}]
```

If you just want the derivative numerically, `ND` is another option:

```wl
Needs["NumericalCalculus`"];
Plot[ND[T[t], t, tt], {tt, 0, 1}]
```

For more complex integrals, the first approach is usually faster.

