`If` only executes the first branch when the condition evaluates to `True`.  
If the condition stays unevaluated because it contains an undefined symbol like `notDefined`, then it is neither `True` nor `False`, so `If` does nothing.

Use the 4th argument of `If` to catch this case:

```mathematica
x = 1;
For[i = 1, i <= 3, i++,
  If[x + i > notDefined, x = 100, , Throw["myError"]];
  Print[StringJoin["i = ", ToString[i], ", x = ", ToString[x]]];
]
```

This will signal an error when the condition cannot be evaluated.

So the behavior is expected: undefined symbols do not automatically produce an error in `If`; they leave the condition unevaluated.

