`Return` only exits the nearest enclosing construct by default. In your case, that’s likely the inner `Do`/`If`/`Block`-style construct, not the `Module`, so execution continues after it.

To force exiting the `Module`, give `Return` the target head explicitly:

```mathematica
MyMod := Module[{i},
  Do[
    Return[1, Module],
    {i, 3}
  ];
  Print["test"]
]
```

Then `MyMod` returns `1` and `Print["test"]` is skipped.

So the key point is: `Return` needs its second argument when you want to jump out of a specific enclosing construct.

