Yes—fit ODE coefficients the same way you would with `NMinimize`: define an error function between the numerical ODE solution and your data, then minimize it over the unknown coefficients.

Typical pattern:

```mathematica
ySol[a_, b_] := NDSolveValue[
  {y'[t] == a - t + b y[t], y[0] == 1},
  y, {t, 0, 1}
];

err[a_, b_] := Total[(ySol[a, b] /@ data[[All, 1]] - data[[All, 2]])^2];

NMinimize[err[a, b], {a, b}]
```

If you have multiple equations, use `NDSolveValue` for the system and build the squared-error sum over all observed variables.

If you share your ODE and data structure, I can write the exact fitting code.

Use the ODE solver inside an optimization over the coefficients, exactly as in your previous case.

For example:

```wl
fit = ParametricNDSolveValue[
  {
    x'[t] == a x[t] - b x[t] y[t],
    y'[t] == -c y[t] + d x[t] y[t],
    x[0] == x0, y[0] == y0
  },
  {x, y}, {t, 0, tmax},
  {a, b, c, d, x0, y0}
];

obj[{aa_, bb_, cc_, dd_, x0v_, y0v_}] := Module[{sol},
  sol = fit[aa, bb, cc, dd, x0v, y0v];
  Total[(sol[[1]] /@ tvals - xvals)^2 + (sol[[2]] /@ tvals - yvals)^2]
];

NMinimize[obj[{a, b, c, d, x0, y0}], {a, b, c, d, x0, y0}]
```

If you only want coefficients, keep initial conditions fixed. For best results, use good initial guesses and bounds. `FindFit` can also be used when the model is straightforward.

