To make a 3D phase portrait with direction fields and sample solution curves in Mathematica, the cleanest route is:

- use `VectorPlot3D` for the direction field
- use `ParametricPlot3D` for a few solution trajectories
- avoid singular points or sample around them with a small offset

Example skeleton:

```mathematica
vf[{x_, y_, z_}] := {f1[x, y, z], f2[x, y, z], f3[x, y, z]};

field = VectorPlot3D[
  vf[{x, y, z}],
  {x, xmin, xmax}, {y, ymin, ymax}, {z, zmin, zmax},
  VectorPoints -> Fine,
  VectorScale -> {Small, Scaled[0.4], None}
];

sol = NDSolve[
  {
    x'[t] == f1[x[t], y[t], z[t]],
    y'[t] == f2[x[t], y[t], z[t]],
    z'[t] == f3[x[t], y[t], z[t]],
    x[0] == x0, y[0] == y0, z[0] == z0
  },
  {x, y, z}, {t, 0, tmax}
];

traj = ParametricPlot3D[
  Evaluate[{x[t], y[t], z[t]} /. sol],
  {t, 0, tmax},
  PlotStyle -> {Red, Thick}
];

Show[field, traj]
```

